---
abstract: We measure how closely lantern moths beat their wings in phase. Across forty colonies, synchrony rises with colony size, and it peaks at dusk.
title: Wingbeat synchrony in captive lantern moth colonies
---

# Introduction

Lantern moths gather in dusk swarms whose wingbeats lock together (Okafor 2017). Why they synchronize is unknown. Phase locking has been measured in other insects (Lindqvist and Moreau 2019).

# Results

Larger colonies beat more nearly in phase than small ones (Figure 1). The order parameter is lowest at dusk across every colony.

The dusk sweep of 2021 repeated the size series under red light; its recordings are kept in data/dusk-sweep-2021.csv and gave R = 0.71.

$$R = |\frac{1}{N}\sum exp(i\varphi)|$$

Figure 1. Order parameter R against colony size, forty colonies.

# Discussion

Our central finding is that synchrony rises with colony size. Dusk light may be the cue.

# Methods

Wingbeats were filmed at 2000 frames per second; the phase of each moth was taken from the analytic signal, as described in methods.tex.

